  "query_preview": "/THURSDAY NIGHT THROUGH TUESDAY/...\nIssued at 150 PM EDT Wed Sep 16 2026\n\nStalled sfc boundary will drift southward towards the Ohio Valley \nand the Commonwealth Thursday night into Friday morning. This will \nincrease shower/storm chances across the CWA especially over \nsouthern IN, north central KY and over the Bluegrass Region. Current \nforecasts continues to show a 20-30 percent chance of PoPs. There \nremains some uncertainty on how far south this boundary will go \nacross the state of KY. The placement of this boundary will have a \nlarge influence on not just potential shower and storm potential but \nalso on our temperatures. The is some guidance that is starting to \nsuggest the boundary could go as far south as the KY/TN border. If \nthis scenario plays out we would not need to continue the Heat \nAdvisory into Friday. If it stops short and makes it to only central \nKY then some of our counties to the south and west may need to \ncontinue the Heat Advisory for another day. Given the lack of \nconfidence, it was decided to wait on more data and confidence \nbefore extending the current Heat Advisory for Friday. \n\nFront will lift north again to start the weekend but the center of \nthe upper ridge will be further west. While we will see temperatures \nboth Saturday and Sunday return to the low-mid 90s, heat index \nvalues will not be as hot. The forecast continues to look mainly dry \nfor the weekend. \n\nPattern flattens out and becomes more zonal for early next week with \na series of systems working through the region with a cold front \npushing through during the first half of the week. This should bring \ncooler temperatures in the 80s by the middle of next week with some \nshowers and possible storms.",
